                <pb facs="0883" n="871" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg871"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">622.116/121: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Long</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-08-21T18:00:00-04:00">August 21, 1940—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>118. Your 188, August 19, 6 p.m. According to our information, all shipments to
                    Ecuador except government orders have been held up since Monday.<anchor n="58"
                        xml:id="d1017-f1"/> This situation is costly and confusing, and will become
                    more so unless clarified promptly. Please emphasize the seriousness of this
                    situation in accordance with Department’s 116 August 17 and endeavor to persuade
                    the Ecuadoran authorities to issue instructions to consuls to certify shipments
                    as heretofore pending clarification of the whole matter.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
